WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:21.000
The first interview this morning is with Rabbi Yosef Faur, PhD, a world-renowned author and lecturer, professor and scholar of Sephardic heritage, culture and law.

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:29.300
He is a Talmud professor at Bar-Ilan University, and let's say good morning to Rabbi Yosef

00:00:29.300 --> 00:00:29.700
Faur.

00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:30.440
Good morning, Rabbi.

00:00:30.760 --> 00:00:31.380
Good morning.

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:37.400
What is the reason that you came here to Florida, all the way from Bar-Ilan University in Ramat

00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:37.580
Gan?

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:43.900
Well, the Koled Haram Bam was very kindly inviting me to deliver a series of lectures

00:00:43.900 --> 00:00:49.000
on very relevant subjects, I believe, and I accepted the invitation.

00:00:49.580 --> 00:00:50.760
Sounds good to me.

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:56.840
Right to me also and I'll be there. I promise you on the foot and the first question is the second one

00:00:56.840 --> 00:01:02.120
I should say I understand you've written there to be Jewish is a very interesting subject

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:08.200
To be Jewish is to be modern and to be modern is to be Jewish. What does it mean?

00:01:08.680 --> 00:01:11.320
Well, I mean to say like this, you know the

00:01:12.540 --> 00:01:14.540
concept of modernity

00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:15.960
that

00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:19.700
Is spread in secular society means to be without a past

00:01:19.700 --> 00:01:25.580
to be aware of the future and to care only about the right now moment. I think that

00:01:25.580 --> 00:01:31.060
that type of modernity is very superficial, does not fulfill an individual.

00:01:31.060 --> 00:01:39.740
In Judaism we have a past and that past in a sense give a certain outline how to

00:01:39.740 --> 00:01:47.640
live. We have a future in the sense we all want the same goal and the challenge

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:49.500
is how to apply it,

00:01:49.980 --> 00:01:51.400
how to apply the past,

00:01:51.800 --> 00:01:53.520
how to structure our present

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:55.660
in terms of

00:01:55.660 --> 00:01:57.280
our history and our

00:01:57.280 --> 00:01:59.080
future.

00:01:59.960 --> 00:02:01.660
Therefore, to be truly a Jew,

00:02:01.880 --> 00:02:03.620
you have to be modern, because you have to know

00:02:03.620 --> 00:02:05.460
how to apply the

00:02:05.460 --> 00:02:08.060
legacy, the traditions of

00:02:08.060 --> 00:02:09.880
Judaism to the

00:02:09.880 --> 00:02:11.800
specific situation

00:02:11.800 --> 00:02:13.940
in which you live. And therefore,

00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:15.580
to be modern

00:02:15.580 --> 00:02:17.200
is to be Jewish also.

00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:24.240
Because the modernity of secular outlook prevents you of having roots.

00:02:24.940 --> 00:02:27.480
And therefore, that modernity is a very shallow one.

00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:31.660
To be a true modern individual, you have to have roots.

00:02:32.280 --> 00:02:41.200
And roots means Judaism, means the scripture, means also a commitment and an outlook for what you want to be in the future.

00:02:41.700 --> 00:02:42.660
Makes sense to me.

00:02:42.940 --> 00:02:43.240
Thank you.

00:02:43.240 --> 00:02:46.160
He also wrote a lot about Sephardic history and heritage.

00:02:46.540 --> 00:02:49.600
In your view, what does it mean to be Sephardic?

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:53.700
Well, let me begin by saying what does not mean to be Sephardic.

00:02:53.800 --> 00:02:54.780
Be Ashkenazi, do you mean?

00:02:54.840 --> 00:02:55.200
No, no.

00:02:55.400 --> 00:02:56.320
I'm kidding, I'm kidding.

00:02:57.640 --> 00:03:00.020
Sephardic is not an ethnicity.

00:03:01.180 --> 00:03:07.220
Some of the greatest scholars that contributed to the Sephardic development were Ashkenazi.

00:03:07.220 --> 00:03:20.800
The Rosh is one of the most outstanding Jewish minds that Judaism has, was born in Germany, but it became a central figure in the Sephardic tradition.

00:03:21.420 --> 00:03:26.820
So, Sephardi is nothing to do with ethnicity.

00:03:27.520 --> 00:03:28.960
It is an outlook.

00:03:29.440 --> 00:03:31.880
Namely, it is a challenge.

00:03:31.880 --> 00:03:34.700
the presupposition is

00:03:34.700 --> 00:03:37.420
that you could be a full-fledged Jew,

00:03:38.540 --> 00:03:40.080
be entirely Jewish

00:03:40.080 --> 00:03:42.900
in a non-Jewish society,

00:03:43.460 --> 00:03:45.900
that you don't have to cheat on your Judaism

00:03:45.900 --> 00:03:49.100
or on the secular society in which you live.

00:03:49.620 --> 00:03:50.700
In our case,

00:03:51.200 --> 00:03:53.920
it means that you could be 100% American

00:03:53.920 --> 00:03:57.140
and 100% Jew at the same time.

00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:00.280
And being Jewish would make you a better American

00:04:00.280 --> 00:04:01.720
from the Sephardic point of view.

00:04:01.880 --> 00:04:05.580
And being an American, it could make you better too.

00:04:06.380 --> 00:04:09.300
So this is what the Sephardic tradition teaches you,

00:04:09.840 --> 00:04:14.260
that you could live in a non-Jewish world,

00:04:14.620 --> 00:04:17.200
share in some of their values,

00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:20.620
and at the same time be 100% true.

00:04:21.180 --> 00:04:24.280
And this is, for me, the most important message

00:04:24.280 --> 00:04:29.200
that the Sephardic experience has to teach us today for us in America.

00:04:29.200 --> 00:04:30.060
I understand.

00:04:30.060 --> 00:04:34.920
now as Americans, I mean they live here, especially the American Jews, is there anything valuable

00:04:34.920 --> 00:04:37.000
we can learn from the Sephardic experience?

00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:43.000
Yes. Well, one of the things that is very important, the Sephardic belief, because,

00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:48.560
two things. Number one is that Spain was a unique country, because it was a pluralistic

00:04:48.560 --> 00:04:56.240
society in the following sense. A Jew in Germany, or a Jew in France or in Poland, he was the

00:04:56.240 --> 00:05:03.600
only minority. The only were Christians and Jews. The Jew was the only minority. In Spain,

00:05:03.860 --> 00:05:08.980
there were Jews, there were Muslims, and there were Christians. Therefore, the Jew was not

00:05:08.980 --> 00:05:14.200
the only minority. It was the beginning of what we maybe call a pluralistic society.

00:05:14.940 --> 00:05:21.640
And the Sephardic tradition teaches you a kind of strategy to survive in the pluralistic

00:05:21.640 --> 00:05:28.760
society. The Jew in Separat had to write in Arabic in the Moorish time, later on in what is Spanish.

00:05:29.320 --> 00:05:37.080
They learned the language of the country, tried to understand the values, and structure

00:05:37.080 --> 00:05:44.280
your Judaism in terms of that language, of that values. In other words, you do not have to erect

00:05:44.280 --> 00:05:46.960
a kind of psychological

00:05:46.960 --> 00:05:49.680
barrier

00:05:49.680 --> 00:05:51.720
yes, of language

00:05:51.720 --> 00:05:52.680
or

00:05:52.680 --> 00:05:54.640
mannerisms

00:05:54.640 --> 00:05:55.960
vis-a-vis the boy

00:05:55.960 --> 00:05:59.060
you could accept some of the values

00:05:59.060 --> 00:06:01.860
and you could speak the same language

00:06:01.860 --> 00:06:03.760
you could go to the same schools

00:06:03.760 --> 00:06:05.060
you could read

00:06:05.060 --> 00:06:06.220
the same books

00:06:06.220 --> 00:06:07.180
and yet

00:06:07.180 --> 00:06:10.780
develop a strategy

00:06:10.780 --> 00:06:12.700
how to be Jewish

00:06:12.700 --> 00:06:13.540
and to preserve

00:06:13.540 --> 00:06:16.480
Jewishness.

00:06:16.700 --> 00:06:18.500
Not only to preserve it, to enhance it.

00:06:19.640 --> 00:06:20.760
Now, the same thing

00:06:20.760 --> 00:06:22.960
eventually will be done

00:06:22.960 --> 00:06:24.460
in order to support in America.

00:06:24.460 --> 00:06:25.660
We cannot erect

00:06:25.660 --> 00:06:27.380
a wall.

00:06:27.980 --> 00:06:30.360
We cannot say to our children, do not speak

00:06:30.360 --> 00:06:32.340
English. We cannot prevent them

00:06:32.340 --> 00:06:34.520
from going to schools. They have to go

00:06:34.520 --> 00:06:35.860
to have an education.

00:06:36.580 --> 00:06:38.380
Now, if this is the case,

00:06:38.960 --> 00:06:40.340
strange enough, America

00:06:40.340 --> 00:06:42.420
will be very similar to the

00:06:42.420 --> 00:06:48.060
problem facing a Jew in Granada or Cordoba in the Middle Ages where he had to learn the

00:06:48.060 --> 00:06:53.800
language of the country in order to survive. So the experience that we had, the Jewish

00:06:53.800 --> 00:07:03.160
people had in Spain, it's very, very similar to the problems challenging us in America.

00:07:03.880 --> 00:07:11.960
And there we were able to solve the problems without intermarriage and without forfeiting

00:07:11.960 --> 00:07:15.780
are very specific characters, I suppose.

00:07:16.640 --> 00:07:17.120
I understand.

00:07:17.320 --> 00:07:21.200
And now we come to the purpose you gave here to Florida, to Miami,

00:07:21.760 --> 00:07:23.380
about the lecture, the Kulela Rambam,

00:07:23.880 --> 00:07:26.380
the series of lectures.

00:07:27.140 --> 00:07:28.760
What are these lectures all about?

00:07:29.100 --> 00:07:29.900
Let me tell you.

00:07:30.100 --> 00:07:34.280
And it's interesting because we're having a lecture,

00:07:34.280 --> 00:07:36.600
and one is on David Nieto.

00:07:37.160 --> 00:07:38.060
Interesting thing.

00:07:38.580 --> 00:07:40.460
David Nieto was from Venice, Italy.

00:07:41.960 --> 00:07:48.960
background was Spanish and Portuguese. He was a member of the Bedin, a superb Dayan.

00:07:48.960 --> 00:07:55.960
Goes to England. When he's in England, he becomes a friend with Isaac Newton, and he influences Isaac Newton. I wrote about it.

00:07:55.960 --> 00:08:06.960
Now, he was a great physician, a superb Dayan, one of the most erudite rabbis of the time.

00:08:06.960 --> 00:08:14.640
And at the same time, he writes his parish about problems of assimilation.

00:08:15.360 --> 00:08:19.600
He was writing for those Jews who remain still in Spain or in Portugal

00:08:19.600 --> 00:08:22.400
and were about to come back to Judaism.

00:08:22.980 --> 00:08:30.320
And therefore, he tries to explain the Torah according to Western schemes of thought.

00:08:30.960 --> 00:08:34.700
And in that sense, he has a wonderful, he has many books.

00:08:34.700 --> 00:08:45.160
One of them is Matedan, in which he explains the whole rabbinic tradition in terms of a historiography which is clearly understood by a Western mind.

00:08:45.600 --> 00:08:51.120
And in that sense, I thought that was interesting because we are Westerners in a sense.

00:08:51.500 --> 00:08:52.940
We live in the United States.

00:08:53.500 --> 00:09:00.020
And here we have a great rabbi teaching us Yahdut in terms that will make sense to us.

00:09:00.020 --> 00:09:04.600
The other author that we study is Moshe Haim Nusakha.

00:09:04.700 --> 00:09:06.820
also from Italy.

00:09:07.700 --> 00:09:08.160
Now,

00:09:08.900 --> 00:09:10.140
Musa Haim Nusratu represents

00:09:10.140 --> 00:09:12.020
not the Sephardic tradition,

00:09:12.700 --> 00:09:14.000
not the Ashkenazi tradition,

00:09:14.460 --> 00:09:15.760
but the Italian tradition.

00:09:16.640 --> 00:09:18.580
And he's the author of one of the most

00:09:18.580 --> 00:09:19.840
beloved books

00:09:19.840 --> 00:09:21.940
in contemporary Jewish,

00:09:22.240 --> 00:09:23.080
Mechilat Yisharim.

00:09:23.720 --> 00:09:25.360
The purpose of that book

00:09:25.360 --> 00:09:28.060
is forward introspection,

00:09:28.220 --> 00:09:28.920
look into yourself,

00:09:29.660 --> 00:09:30.740
organize your life,

00:09:31.200 --> 00:09:34.260
vis-a-vis the ultimate end of all men,

00:09:34.260 --> 00:09:40.900
all humans. And since we are right before Rosh Hashanah Yom Kippur, a time of introspection,

00:09:41.460 --> 00:09:48.900
I thought that the whole theme of Mitzvah Tisharim, repentance, spiritual re-edification,

00:09:52.500 --> 00:09:57.620
looking at your bearings, knowing where you're coming from and where you're going at,

00:09:57.620 --> 00:10:03.160
that's so Jewish, it's particularly timely in the month of Elul.

00:10:03.700 --> 00:10:06.700
So this is the second subject we're going to study.

00:10:07.000 --> 00:10:10.220
Mesila Tessarim, Moshe Haim Lusato, a Westerner, by the way,

00:10:10.500 --> 00:10:12.920
Italy, fairly Jewish,

00:10:13.020 --> 00:10:18.340
the Gaon may be not thought about as one of the great minds of the time,

00:10:18.900 --> 00:10:23.540
and David Nieto, a great Dayan, a great halakhist,

00:10:23.540 --> 00:10:28.220
a scholar, a physician, an astronomer who lived in England,

00:10:28.360 --> 00:10:30.700
was the chief rabbi of the Sephardim in England,

00:10:31.100 --> 00:10:33.440
and he wrote many books, among them Martedan,

00:10:33.780 --> 00:10:38.600
which is how to be Jewish in our own Western world.

00:10:39.260 --> 00:10:41.520
That makes, I think, a lot of sense,

00:10:41.520 --> 00:10:43.720
and I hope people will come to Kolela Rambam,

00:10:43.840 --> 00:10:47.480
which is located at 7800 Esplanola Avenue in North Bay Village.

00:10:48.400 --> 00:10:51.600
And Rabbi Yusef Aour, I hope to see you soon.

00:10:51.600 --> 00:10:53.620
and of course we're going to have another interview

00:10:53.620 --> 00:10:55.400
in the Hebrew section and then in the Spanish.

00:10:55.900 --> 00:10:57.320
Wonderful. I thank you very much.

00:10:57.420 --> 00:10:59.280
And I wish you a happy and a healthy new year.

00:10:59.620 --> 00:11:00.900
All the best. Thank you.

00:11:21.600 --> 00:11:29.140
52231080, 52231080, and in all dry areas, the radio will also be broadcast in Spanish later.

00:11:30.040 --> 00:11:33.120
So, Rabbi, let's talk to the Rabbi.

00:11:34.020 --> 00:11:35.420
Kavod HaRab, good morning to you.

00:11:35.840 --> 00:11:36.860
Good morning, blessed.

00:11:38.380 --> 00:11:39.260
What brought you to what you call to do the

00:11:39.260 --> 00:11:41.260
What is the purpose of the Jewish-Masovian tradition?

00:11:41.260 --> 00:12:06.160
Rabbi Abraham Tzalem invited me, he has a

00:12:06.160 --> 00:12:17.160
If we have not only a tradition of the past, but a tradition that forces us to deal with our past, with our specific past.

00:12:17.160 --> 00:12:32.160
And to be a true Jew, we need to know how to translate and translate our past, our past, to our specific data today.

00:12:32.160 --> 00:12:44.060
We are modern. And we are more modern than anyone else, because the secular modernism is a modernism without the past, without the future.

00:12:44.760 --> 00:12:47.460
And for this, it is a modernism that is spiritual.

00:12:48.140 --> 00:12:57.900
The real modernism is one who knows how to deal not only with the past, but also with the past, and also he thinks about his future.

00:12:57.900 --> 00:12:59.900
to the future.

00:12:59.900 --> 00:13:01.900
I understand. You also wrote

00:13:01.900 --> 00:13:03.900
many articles about the culture of the Sephardic heritage.

00:13:03.900 --> 00:13:05.900
What is your intention

00:13:05.900 --> 00:13:07.900
to be Sephardic?

00:13:07.900 --> 00:13:09.900
First of all, to be Sephardic is not an ethnic thing.

00:13:09.900 --> 00:13:11.900
It is a tradition.

00:13:11.900 --> 00:13:13.900
It is a tradition, a habit.

00:13:13.900 --> 00:13:15.900
This habit is a very

00:13:15.900 --> 00:13:17.900
optimistic habit that teaches us

00:13:17.900 --> 00:13:19.900
that the Jewish

00:13:19.900 --> 00:13:21.900
and the Jewish community

00:13:21.900 --> 00:13:23.900
have something to say

00:13:23.900 --> 00:13:25.900
to the general culture,

00:13:25.900 --> 00:13:32.900
and also that it is possible to manage a Turkish culture with a non-Jewish culture.

00:13:32.900 --> 00:13:42.900
That is why we have people in the past, like the Rambam and others, who were great, not only in Judaism, but also in the culture of the people.

00:13:42.900 --> 00:13:49.900
And therefore they knew how to be Jewish and also a general woman culture.

00:13:49.900 --> 00:13:53.900
foreign

00:14:03.900 --> 00:14:05.900
foreign

00:14:19.900 --> 00:14:24.620
Now, something that I would like to know, and what would you like to say in short?

00:14:24.780 --> 00:14:26.540
I know it can be talked about for hours.

00:14:27.420 --> 00:14:30.520
The difference between the Sephardic leadership and the Ashkenazi leadership,

00:14:30.720 --> 00:14:34.220
in the sense that we are all living in one country now, in the State of Israel,

00:14:34.540 --> 00:14:37.060
and our intention is to be united at some stage.

00:14:37.720 --> 00:14:38.820
Is there a chance that this will happen?

00:14:38.820 --> 00:14:43.660
I think that not only is there a chance that this will happen, but that it is already beginning.

00:14:43.660 --> 00:14:50.660
I think that all the differences between the Sephardim and Ashkenazim who were in Gola

00:14:50.660 --> 00:14:52.660
are not the same today in Israel.

00:14:52.660 --> 00:14:56.660
I can go to the Knesset and feel like a Sephardim.

00:14:56.660 --> 00:14:58.660
The expression is like a Sephardim.

00:14:58.660 --> 00:15:06.660
And there are many, many Jewish-Sephardim who entered the Israeli culture

00:15:06.660 --> 00:15:11.660
and there are also some of the Yafah-Ashkenazim who entered the Sephardim culture.

00:15:11.660 --> 00:15:17.740
I think that today every Jew has something from these two traditions, Sephardi and Ashkenazi.

00:15:19.260 --> 00:15:20.580
Especially in the Jewish community.

00:15:21.000 --> 00:15:23.000
Yes, is there a chance that there will be a time, like now,

00:15:23.080 --> 00:15:27.500
where there is a Sephardi Rashi Bakshi and an Ashkenazi Rav Lau,

00:15:27.960 --> 00:15:33.100
is there a chance that there will be a time when there will be one and only rabbinic ministry for the Jewish people?

00:15:33.100 --> 00:15:37.080
I think it's not just possible, we need to expect that there will be one.

00:15:37.080 --> 00:15:37.420
Absolutely.

00:15:37.840 --> 00:15:38.520
Absolutely, absolutely.

00:15:38.520 --> 00:15:47.520
If possible, that is not logical to say that there are two different versions of the Jews.

00:15:47.520 --> 00:15:53.520
We are one nation, we believe in the same system, and our children unite with each other.

00:15:53.520 --> 00:15:56.520
And we are taking a lesson from this and that.

00:15:56.520 --> 00:15:57.520
All the glory.

00:15:57.520 --> 00:16:01.520
Yes, I would not want to be like the Christians, who have Catholics and Protestants.

00:16:01.520 --> 00:16:04.520
I think it is a destructive peace.

00:16:04.520 --> 00:16:06.520
I agree 100%.

00:16:06.520 --> 00:16:07.880
The next question is, as an American, is there anything in the world that we here in America can learn from the Zephyr

00:16:07.880 --> 00:16:18.880
Yes, for example, when the first Zephyr came to the United States, and also to South America,

00:16:18.880 --> 00:16:29.880
from the first who came here, they took part, they knew how to take part with the non-Jews, the Christians.

00:16:29.880 --> 00:16:41.780
For example, one of the students of Columbia University was the Rabbi Gershon Seishas Mendez, the Rabbi of the University of New York.

00:16:41.780 --> 00:16:50.280
They were impressed by the education, because they knew that if we had good education for non-Jews, it would also benefit Jews.

00:16:50.280 --> 00:16:55.960
For example, in the political sphere of America,

00:16:56.400 --> 00:17:00.300
Gershon Seychelles Mendez was a friend of George Washington.

00:17:00.720 --> 00:17:04.100
David Nassif was a friend of Benjamin Franklin.

00:17:04.580 --> 00:17:06.620
They saw themselves as Jews,

00:17:07.120 --> 00:17:11.040
that they could be good Americans because they were Jews,

00:17:11.620 --> 00:17:13.680
and good Jews because they were Americans.

00:17:14.000 --> 00:17:17.700
Right, and the one who led the revolution against the British was a Jew.

00:17:18.040 --> 00:17:18.260
Right.

00:17:18.260 --> 00:17:19.000
His name was Solomon.

00:17:19.200 --> 00:17:19.360
Right.

00:17:19.360 --> 00:17:21.360
Haim Solomon.

00:17:21.360 --> 00:17:28.360
They saw themselves as people who lived in two cultures at the same time.

00:17:28.360 --> 00:17:37.360
They had Jewish culture and American culture, and they didn't see any contradiction between the two until the end.

00:17:37.360 --> 00:17:38.360
There were no contradictions at all.

00:17:38.360 --> 00:17:39.360
No contradictions, yes.

00:17:39.360 --> 00:17:44.360
And I think to this day that I am a better Jew than I am living in America.

00:17:44.360 --> 00:17:45.360
I am American.

00:17:45.360 --> 00:17:54.360
And I, as an American, believe that the same American is the origin of the United States.

00:17:54.360 --> 00:17:56.360
Yes, at least you know that you are a Christian.

00:17:56.360 --> 00:18:00.360
There are things that the United States...

00:18:00.360 --> 00:18:07.360
I have been trying to learn that those who came, what we call the founding fathers of the United States,

